                <pb facs="0886" n="774" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg774"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363/121: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-01T14:00:00-05:00">March 1, 1935—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>59. No. 39, February 25, 4 p.m. from Minister at Nanking. The Department desires
                    that the Legation take appropriate steps orally and informally to bring to the
                    personal attention of the Minister of Industry and of Mr. Wicks the general
                    policy of the American Government in regard to monopolies in China, pointing out
                    that, although the Department is not in position to pass upon in advance any
                    particular concession or proposal without having all the facts before it, this
                    proposal, as reported in the above mentioned telegram, would appear clearly to
                    contravene Article 3 of the Nine Power Treaty of February 6, 1922, to which both
                    the American and Chinese Governments, among other governments, are signatories
                    and that in the circumstances the American Government would be [forced to
                    disapprove] the proposal.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
